**Design:** a queue protected by one `std::mutex`, with a `std::condition_variable` for "not empty" (and, if bounded, one for "not full").

```cpp
template <class T>
class BlockingQueue {
    std::queue<T> q; size_t cap;
    std::mutex m; std::condition_variable notEmpty, notFull;
    bool closed = false;
public:
    explicit BlockingQueue(size_t c) : cap(c) {}
    void push(T v) {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lk(m);
        notFull.wait(lk, [&]{ return q.size() < cap || closed; });
        if (closed) return;
        q.push(std::move(v));
        notEmpty.notify_one();
    }
    bool pop(T& out) {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lk(m);
        notEmpty.wait(lk, [&]{ return !q.empty() || closed; });
        if (q.empty()) return false;        // closed and drained
        out = std::move(q.front()); q.pop();
        notFull.notify_one();
        return true;
    }
    void close() {
        { std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lk(m); closed = true; }
        notEmpty.notify_all(); notFull.notify_all();
    }
};
```

**Key points:**
- `wait` atomically releases the mutex and blocks, then reacquires it on wakeup. Always wait with a **predicate** (a loop) because of spurious wakeups and because another consumer may take the item first.
- Notify after changing state; `notify_one` is enough for one item.

**The follow-up:** if all consumers are already waiting and no producer has started, nothing is wrong. The consumers are **blocked inside `wait`, consuming no CPU** (not spinning), because the predicate `!q.empty()` is false. When a producer later pushes and calls `notify_one`, one consumer wakes, rechecks the predicate, takes the item and continues. A notification sent before anyone waits is not lost here because the **predicate is based on the shared state, not on the signal**; this is exactly why the predicate is checked under the lock before waiting. The real hazard is a program that never produces: the consumers would wait forever, which is why you provide `close()` or a stop flag so they can exit cleanly.

**Alternatives:** lock-free queues for high throughput, and semaphores.
